  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Manny Machado drove in three runs in a 5-1 win over the Dodgers. The offense clicked early and the bullpen locked down for 5.2 scoreless innings. Down nine in the division with fifteen games left, this felt like the kind of night that either becomes a run or stays isolated.",
    "what_this_means": "Machado converted runners on base into runs. The job that separates production from presence. The lineup touched the Dodgers for eight hits, but what mattered was when those hits came: they scored in the fourth to take the lead, added two more in the fifth to push it to four. The bullpen. Morgan, Matsui, Rodriguez, Morejon, Miller. Didn't need to hold much after that. Rodriguez struck out three in two scoreless. It was clean. They never trailed, never recovered from anything because there was nothing to recover from. This is what balance looks like: the lineup doesn't have to carry, and the bullpen doesn't have to survive. They just have to show up in the same inning.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tyler Glasnow takes the ball tonight for LA with a 3.46 ERA this season. Nick Pivetta's last four starts carry a 1.59 ERA. If that line holds, this becomes a different kind of game.",
